## The main session plans; helpers do the work
The main session runs on the most expensive model. It only understands the request,
plans, delegates, judges results and answers. It does **not** read code, search, edit
files or run commands itself, however small the job: every hands-on step goes to a
cheaper helper. The one exception: reading up to ~30 lines a helper cited, to verify it.

| Job | Helper | Model |
|---|---|---|
| Find code, read files, search, audit | `explorer` | Haiku |
| Run tests, commands, git status/diff | `runner` | Haiku |
| Edit files (split work over 2+ files into several calls) | `builder` | Sonnet |
| Root cause of a failure | `debugger` | Sonnet |
| Web and docs lookups | `researcher` | Sonnet |
| Review a diff over ~30 lines or touching security, data or money | `reviewer` | Haiku |
| Costly-to-reverse decision, or two failed attempts | `big-decision` | Fable |

Give each helper a precise task with paths, so it doesn't re-explore. Independent
helper calls go in one message, so they run in parallel.

## Every change
- Do only what was asked, in the fewest lines that fully solve it.
- Before adding a file, function or dependency, have `explorer` check one doesn't exist.
- After a change, have `runner` run the narrowest test that proves it. Two failed fixes → `debugger`.
- Unfamiliar area: helpers read `CODEMAP.md` first. Run `/map` at the end only if files
  were added, moved or deleted.
- Replies: the answer, then at most one short follow-up line.
- Same correction twice, or "always/never": add a one-line rule to `.claude/LEARNED.md`.
- Never read or print secrets. Never commit `.env`, keys or tokens.

